course: dictionary
lesson: 11895
title: One word, two meanings: brook
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A brook is a small stream of fresh water. It is smaller than a river and often runs through fields and woods. «We sat on the bank and watched the brook tumble over the stones.» The words bank, stones and tumble all tell you it is water running along the ground.
explain: Brook can also be a verb, but it is formal and found mostly in older books. It is nearly always used in a negative sentence, and it means to allow or put up with something. «The headteacher would brook no talking in the hall» means she would not allow any talking at all.
explain: To find the right meaning, look at the words around the word. If you can see water, fields or woods, it is the noun. If someone is refusing to allow something, it is the verb. Swap in stream or allow and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A brook is a small stream of fresh water.
fact: A brook is smaller than a river.
fact: Poems often describe a brook as babbling, which means making a soft, bubbling sound.
fact: As a verb, brook is formal: «The captain would brook no delay» means the captain would allow no delay.
fact: Brook is usually used in a negative sentence, such as «brook no interference».
exercise: «We paddled our feet in the cold brook behind the farm.» What is a brook? | A big ocean wave | A type of wooden bridge | A tall hill covered in trees | *A small stream of fresh water
exercise: «The teacher would brook no talking during the test.» What does brook mean here? | To fetch something from the cupboard | To swim across quickly | *To allow or put up with
exercise: «The cows drank from the clear water of the stream.» Find and copy one word that means a small stream. | pond | river | *brook | lake
test: «The brook babbled gently over the pebbles.» What does babbled suggest? | It was frozen solid | It stopped flowing completely | It flooded the field | *It made a soft, bubbling sound
test: «The old lord would brook no excuses from his servants.» What does brook no excuses mean? | *He would not allow any excuses | He had lost all his excuses | He was happy to hear them | He wanted to hear every excuse
exam: Which sentence uses brook as a verb, meaning to allow? | «We sat on the grass beside the brook.» | «The brook ran past the old mill.» | «The brook was narrow and clear.» | *«The head would brook no late arrivals.»
